WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.277
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.277 --> 00:00:12.213
An excellent organization is one where
people do the right thing even when

00:00:12.213 --> 00:00:14.764
nobody's watching them
over their shoulder.

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:17.123
Because that's what
makes you best in class,

00:00:17.123 --> 00:00:19.980
that's actually what makes you innovative.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.000
That's actually what's the foundation for
being a place that employees love to work.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.435
So that's the hardest thing, how do
you build an organization where people

00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:33.052
do the right thing even when
nobody is watching all over them?

00:00:33.052 --> 00:00:38.559
[MUSIC]

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:43.014
Accountability to me is not a list of
metrics, accountability is what you feel,

00:00:43.014 --> 00:00:46.090
so felt accountability
is the important thing.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.468
And from my point of view,
there are two foundations,

00:00:49.468 --> 00:00:52.333
one is a feeling of
psychological ownership,

00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:56.606
the other is a sense of obligation,
and each one balances the other.

00:00:56.606 --> 00:01:00.170
If it's 100% psychological ownership,

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.932
0% obligation,
it becomes a gateway to being egocentric.

00:01:04.932 --> 00:01:07.905
On the other hand if
it's 100% obligation and

00:01:07.905 --> 00:01:11.941
no sense of psychological ownership,
that becomes burdensome and

00:01:11.941 --> 00:01:15.709
therefore unsustainable, so
you need a blend of both things.

00:01:15.709 --> 00:01:21.357
[MUSIC]

00:01:21.357 --> 00:01:25.170
The problem I have with most executives
is all that they think about is geez,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.770
we've got to crank the incentive lever.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:31.280
In short, we gotta pay people more,
the more we pay people,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.610
apparently the more accountable they feel,
I'm not so sure.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.700
Instead, you can actually
do more lightweight things,

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.100
as a matter of fact,
with my colleague Lindy Greer and

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.220
a wonderful graduate
student Jennifer Daniels,

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.472
the three of us did an interesting
experiment with ATT new venture teams.

00:01:48.472 --> 00:01:49.393
What did we do?

00:01:49.393 --> 00:01:52.229
We randomly put these
teams into two conditions,

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:55.410
the control condition which
I won't bother you about.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:59.663
And then a treatment condition, these
teams were teams of four to five people.

00:01:59.663 --> 00:02:04.068
And all that we asked team members
in the treatment condition was,

00:02:04.068 --> 00:02:07.855
choose a job title that
showcases your unique strength.

00:02:07.855 --> 00:02:12.736
And people came up with titles like
guru of software or the queen bee of

00:02:12.736 --> 00:02:18.218
customer service, or the defender of
the code or whatever, it was amazing.

00:02:18.218 --> 00:02:21.680
The teams where people chose their
own job titles what did it do?

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.167
It actually made them feel
less anonymous obviously,

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:28.549
it improved their sense of self-esteem.

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:33.370
But much more importantly, it also solved
the problem of who knows what in the team.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.720
They were able to build
prototypes that were quicker and

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.320
better than others in
the control condition.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:42.652
My question to you is,

00:02:42.652 --> 00:02:46.048
does it cost you anything to ask people
to come up with their own title?

00:02:46.048 --> 00:02:52.216
[MUSIC]

00:02:52.216 --> 00:02:56.964
In most organizations when I look
at high performers, on average,

00:02:56.964 --> 00:03:00.815
high performers can be
categorized into two buckets.

00:03:00.815 --> 00:03:04.679
One bucket is high performers
who are high maintenance,

00:03:04.679 --> 00:03:07.819
they need to be stroked,
they need to be paid,

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:12.667
they need to be recognized, and
they're extremely self-centered.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.858
On the other hand,
there are other high performers who have

00:03:18.858 --> 00:03:23.370
been referred to as extra milers,
and what do they do?

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.732
They perform well, but
they're also the most helpful people.

00:03:26.732 --> 00:03:30.619
They're the go-to people when you run into
a problem, hey how do I deal with this

00:03:30.619 --> 00:03:33.666
customer problem,
how do I deal with the technology problem?

00:03:33.666 --> 00:03:37.637
It's these extra milers that do the most,

00:03:37.637 --> 00:03:43.630
it turns out most executives
they overload the extra-milers.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:45.420
You're never going to go
to your high maintenance,

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.850
high performance, high performer and
say can you please do that.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.670
They'll just immediately want
a lot of things from you,

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:54.420
you instead go to your extra-milers.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:57.260
They're very good at what they do,
what's their principle weakness?

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:00.880
They don't know how to say no to you,
or for that matter anybody.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.502
So ironically the more you overload them,
the more overwhelmed they get.

00:04:05.502 --> 00:04:09.474
And some studies have actually shown
that if you overload them with too many

00:04:09.474 --> 00:04:13.713
requests, they actually begin to develop
intentions of leaving the company.

00:04:13.713 --> 00:04:15.081
Is that what we want to do?

00:04:17.065 --> 00:04:18.066
I think not.

00:04:18.066 --> 00:04:26.612
[MUSIC]

